# Copy this file to .env (gitignored) and set a real password before starting the database.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=Ch4ngeMe!Now

# Role the Postgres container creates on first init. Kept as `collega` so local,
# in-cluster (SPEC/50-kubernetes-deployment.md) and app connection strings all name the
# same role. Note this is still the container's *superuser*, just not called `postgres` —
# the image has no way to create a least-privileged role without an init script. Azure
# deliberately differs (`collegaadmin`), because its managed admin-user model dictates it.
POSTGRES_USER=collega

# Optional: host-side port mapping for the Postgres container.
POSTGRES_HOST_PORT=5432

# The other two parts apps/api composes DATABASE_URL from when that is unset. Both have
# defaults that match the container above, so a local clone needs neither. docker compose
# does NOT read them - it names the database `Collega` and the host is the container.
# POSTGRES_HOST=127.0.0.1
# POSTGRES_DB=Collega

# The bootstrap Site Admin credential (SPEC/20-feature-auth.md requirements 8 and 9). apps/api
# refuses to boot without both, and `pnpm --filter @collega/infrastructure db:bootstrap-admin` is
# what actually creates the account - from these two values, forced to change on first login. The
# demo seed creates it too, but only outside production. Never commit a real password here.
SITE_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@collega.local
SITE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=Ch4ngeMe!Now

# The first organization, for a production deployment that has nothing but a Site Admin - who
# belongs to no organization and has no admin write path in the UI, so the application is otherwise
# unusable (SPEC/50-vercel-deployment.md §8a). Read by
# `pnpm --filter @collega/infrastructure db:bootstrap-organization`, which is run BY HAND, once,
# and deliberately not from the API's build command: it prints an invite code and, when
# BOOTSTRAP_ORG_ADMIN_EMAIL is set, a generated Org Admin password, neither of which belongs in a
# build log. Left unset it exits 0 and creates nothing, so a local clone needs none of them.
# BOOTSTRAP_ORG_TITLE=
# BOOTSTRAP_ORG_DESCRIPTION=
# BOOTSTRAP_ORG_ADMIN_EMAIL=

# The single deployment-level Anthropic key for AI idea assist, shared by every
# organization (SPEC/20-feature-ai-idea-assist.md rule 29). Leave it empty and the feature
# runs dark: the brainstorm falls back to its scripted prompts and the API answers "not
# configured" rather than erroring (rule 31).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# The connection string, and the one place any real credential belongs.
#
# **One name, every environment.** This is the only connection string the app reads, and Vercel
# scopes variables per environment - so the same name holds the production database in Production
# and the staging one in Preview. A storage integration connected with `--prefix` writes
# `<PREFIX>_POSTGRES_URL` instead, which nothing here reads; connect it without one. `.env` is gitignored;
# never put a live URL in this file, in a spec, or in a commit.
#
# The Nest host accepts either this or the POSTGRES_* parts above, composing the URL from the
# parts when this is unset. **The Prisma CLI does not** - `prisma db pull`, `prisma migrate` and
# `prisma studio` read `env("DATABASE_URL")` straight out of the schema and cannot see the parts.
# So set this whenever you intend to run a Prisma command, even locally, and set it verbatim in
# deployment, which is the shape Prisma Postgres hands over.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://collega:<password>@127.0.0.1:5432/Collega?schema=public

# Pointing at Prisma Postgres (Vercel) instead of the local container. Connecting a store
# generates three variables under whatever prefix you gave it - `<PREFIX>_POSTGRES_URL`,
# `<PREFIX>_PRISMA_DATABASE_URL` and `<PREFIX>_DATABASE_URL` - and NOTHING in this repo reads
# any of them. Copy the value whose scheme is `postgres://` or `postgresql://` into
# DATABASE_URL above, which is normally `<PREFIX>_POSTGRES_URL`. That one name is what
# schema.prisma and apps/api both read, and it is the same name in every environment, which is
# the point: the value differs, the name does not.
#
# Do NOT copy a `prisma+postgres://` value. That is the Accelerate protocol, and this workspace
# installs no Accelerate extension - `provider = "postgresql"` with a plain PrismaClient expects
# a TCP URL. Take the direct (non-pooled) one: the schema has a single `url` and no `directUrl`,
# so the same string serves both `prisma migrate` and the runtime. Splitting them would mean
# amending a schema frozen at S0.2, which is a spec question rather than a slice decision.
#
# On Vercel itself the same rule applies to the project, not just this file: apps/api's build
# command runs db:migrate and db:bootstrap-admin, and its host reads DATABASE_URL. A store
# connected under a prefix leaves no plain DATABASE_URL behind, so add one per environment -
# preview pointing at the dev database, production at its own.

# `pnpm dev` refuses to run against a host that is not this machine, because it deploys
# migrations and seeds demo organizations and users into whatever DATABASE_URL names, and the
# seed's only other guard is NODE_ENV=production - which a developer shell does not set. Set
# this to 1 when migrating and seeding a shared dev or preview database is genuinely what you
# meant (tools/local/start.ts).
# COLLEGA_ALLOW_REMOTE_DATABASE=1

# --- apps/api (Nest host, Sprint 9) ---

# Signs the session cookie's JWT (SPEC/decisions.md 2026-09-04, ticket 08). Optional locally -
# a random per-process key is generated if unset, exactly like .NET's Auth:TokenSigningKey - but
# that invalidates every session on restart and is never shared across instances.
#
# REQUIRED when NODE_ENV=production, and the API refuses to boot without it. On Vercel every cold
# start is a new process, so an unset key signs each instance's sessions with a key the others
# reject and users are signed out at random (SPEC/50-vercel-deployment.md). Generate one with
# `openssl rand -base64 48`, and use a different value per environment.
# ACCESS_TOKEN_SIGNING_KEY=

# Minutes before the session cookie's JWT expires. Defaults to 480 (8h), matching .NET's
# Auth:AccessTokenLifetimeMinutes default.
# ACCESS_TOKEN_LIFETIME_MINUTES=480

# apps/api's HTTP port. Defaults to 3001 (apps/web takes Next's default 3000). Vercel injects
# its own PORT in deployment.
# PORT=3001

# VERCEL is injected by the platform ("1" in every Vercel environment) and is READ, NOT SET.
# It is listed here only because it changes behaviour rather than just naming an address: it is
# what tells the API to trust one proxy hop and take the auth rate limiter's caller IP from
# x-forwarded-for. Vercel overwrites that header with the real client address; nothing else does,
# so setting this by hand on a machine that is not behind Vercel would let any caller choose their
# own rate-limit bucket by sending the header themselves. Leave it alone.

# --- apps/web (Next host, Sprint 9) ---

# Where apps/web reaches apps/api, including the /api/v1 prefix. Read on the SERVER ONLY and
# deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_ - the browser never calls the API (apps/web/lib/api/config.ts).
# Defaults to http://127.0.0.1:3001/api/v1, so a local clone needs nothing set. In deployment it
# names the other Vercel project: production points at the production API, previews at the shared
# staging API (SPEC/50-vercel-deployment.md).
# COLLEGA_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3001/api/v1

# Holds every server render open so a `loading.tsx` is visible at all - a local API answers in
# single-digit milliseconds, so no loading state renders without it. Inert when
# NODE_ENV=production, deliberately: a project environment variable is settable by more people
# than a deploy is (apps/web/lib/data/latency.ts).
# MOCK_LATENCY_MS=800

# Comma-separated reader names that should fail, for exercising an `error.tsx` against real
# endpoints - `MOCK_FAIL=getBoards pnpm dev`. Inert in production for the same reason.
# MOCK_FAIL=

# --- tooling ---

# Where `pnpm shots` points the capture browser. Defaults to http://localhost:3000
# (tools/demo-shots/capture.ts).
# COLLEGA_DEMO_URL=http://localhost:3000

# The Chromium the Playwright suite should use when its own download is unreachable. Set by
# `.claude/hooks/session-start.sh` in Claude Code on the web; leave it unset everywhere else,
# where Playwright resolves its own (e2e/playwright.config.ts).
# PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_PATH=
